WEBVTT

00:00:01.50 --> 00:00:05.96
This is journey nine of Hewn, the software factory: recover a failed stage.

00:00:05.96 --> 00:00:06.55
Runs fail.

00:00:06.55 --> 00:00:10.47
A skill's output doesn't validate, a service is down for a minute,

00:00:10.47 --> 00:00:11.66
or a check goes red.

00:00:11.66 --> 00:00:16.24
Most of those failures pass on a second try, so Hewn should retry on its own,

00:00:16.24 --> 00:00:21.23
give up cleanly when it really is stuck, and let an Owner start the stage again once

00:00:21.23 --> 00:00:21.89
it's fixed.

00:00:21.89 --> 00:00:26.52
This video walks through how it was built: the backoff, the retries, blocking,

00:00:26.52 --> 00:00:28.72
Retry stage, the page, and the tests.

00:00:29.87 --> 00:00:33.91
When a run fails, Hewn retries it automatically, up to three times,

00:00:33.91 --> 00:00:37.47
waiting thirty seconds, then two minutes, then ten minutes.

00:00:37.47 --> 00:00:42.17
When the third retry fails too, the stage is Failed and the direction Blocked.

00:00:42.17 --> 00:00:47.11
Mission Control hears about it with the reason and the last two hundred log lines,

00:00:47.11 --> 00:00:49.40
and every other project keeps running.

00:00:49.40 --> 00:00:54.34
Then an Owner presses Retry stage, and the failed stage runs again from its start,

00:00:54.34 --> 00:00:56.09
with its retry counter reset.

00:00:57.24 --> 00:00:58.63
Here is the schedule.

00:00:58.63 --> 00:01:01.87
Attempt one fails, and Hewn waits thirty seconds.

00:01:01.87 --> 00:01:04.78
Attempt two fails, and it waits two minutes.

00:01:04.78 --> 00:01:07.83
Attempt three fails, and it waits ten minutes.

00:01:07.83 --> 00:01:12.52
When attempt four fails, the stage is Failed and the direction Blocked,

00:01:12.52 --> 00:01:14.84
until an Owner presses Retry stage.

00:01:15.99 --> 00:01:18.83
Each retry is a new run of the same stage.

00:01:18.83 --> 00:01:24.43
When a run fails below the limit, the orchestrator queues the next attempt straight

00:01:24.43 --> 00:01:27.74
away, with a not before time: now, plus the wait.

00:01:27.74 --> 00:01:32.60
The run log says which attempt failed, why, and when the next one comes,

00:01:32.60 --> 00:01:35.64
and a retry scheduled event carries the same.

00:01:35.99 --> 00:01:37.80
The lease does the waiting.

00:01:37.80 --> 00:01:42.71
Its query skips any run whose time hasn't come, using the injected clock.

00:01:42.71 --> 00:01:46.27
Workers need no timers, a restart keeps the schedule,

00:01:46.27 --> 00:01:49.77
and other projects' runs are leased in the meantime.

00:01:50.12 --> 00:01:53.30
The first test failed before any of this existed.

00:01:53.30 --> 00:01:57.85
It fails a run, moves the test clock to one second short of each wait,

00:01:57.85 --> 00:02:01.61
and checks that nothing can be leased, then one second on,

00:02:01.61 --> 00:02:03.30
that the next attempt can.

00:02:04.45 --> 00:02:07.30
When attempt four fails, the stage is Failed,

00:02:07.30 --> 00:02:11.48
and the direction and the project are Blocked, holding the reason.

00:02:11.48 --> 00:02:16.67
A stage blocked event carries the reason and the run's last two hundred log lines.

00:02:16.67 --> 00:02:21.41
They were redacted when they were stored, so no secret leaves in the event.

00:02:22.56 --> 00:02:26.81
Retry stage is a new endpoint under the project, for Owners only.

00:02:26.81 --> 00:02:30.79
It refuses anything but a Blocked direction, with a conflict.

00:02:30.79 --> 00:02:34.77
It queues attempt one of the failed stage with no checkpoint,

00:02:34.77 --> 00:02:38.88
so it starts from the beginning, and the log records who asked.

00:02:40.03 --> 00:02:44.76
On the project page, the stage's live line counts down to each retry,

00:02:44.76 --> 00:02:45.99
beside the reason.

00:02:45.99 --> 00:02:51.26
Once the stage blocks, the header shows Blocked, with Retry stage and Cancel,

00:02:51.26 --> 00:02:56.40
and the failed stage opens with a callout: the reason, the waits Hewn used,

00:02:56.40 --> 00:02:59.82
and that the last two hundred log lines are below.

00:02:59.82 --> 00:03:05.30
If the page is open when it happens, a danger toast says the project is blocked.

00:03:05.30 --> 00:03:09.88
It stays until it's dismissed, and offers Open log and Retry stage.

00:03:09.88 --> 00:03:14.88
It works in dark mode and on a phone, and a Viewer sees why it's blocked,

00:03:14.88 --> 00:03:16.25
with no Retry stage.

00:03:17.40 --> 00:03:20.14
Each slice began with its tests failing.

00:03:20.14 --> 00:03:24.79
The backend gained four tests against the real API: the three waits,

00:03:24.79 --> 00:03:29.78
blocking with the last two hundred lines while another project is leased,

00:03:29.78 --> 00:03:34.44
Retry stage from the start with the counter reset, and the refusals.

00:03:34.44 --> 00:03:39.57
The worker's scripted stages can now fail a set number of times, then pass.

00:03:39.92 --> 00:03:43.83
All green, the backend has one hundred and ninety four tests,

00:03:43.83 --> 00:03:48.64
the worker one hundred and forty, and the frontend ninety three unit tests.

00:03:48.64 --> 00:03:53.76
End to end, a new pipeline test runs a real worker whose stage fails four times.

00:03:53.76 --> 00:03:57.42
It watches the countdown, the Blocked page and the toast,

00:03:57.42 --> 00:04:02.29
checks that a Viewer is refused, then presses Retry stage and sees the stage

00:04:02.29 --> 00:04:02.80
succeed.

00:04:02.80 --> 00:04:05.75
There are fifty three end to end tests in all.

00:04:06.90 --> 00:04:08.29
Here is Parcel Post.

00:04:08.29 --> 00:04:10.86
Bootstrap has passed, and Idea fails.

00:04:10.86 --> 00:04:16.07
The live line counts down to retry one of three, with the reason beside it.

00:04:16.07 --> 00:04:20.52
For this recording the waits are five, eight and twelve seconds,

00:04:20.52 --> 00:04:24.34
instead of thirty seconds, two minutes and ten minutes.

00:04:24.34 --> 00:04:29.20
Each retry is a fresh run on the same worker, so its log starts empty,

00:04:29.20 --> 00:04:33.37
and the countdown always shows when the next attempt begins.

00:04:33.37 --> 00:04:37.12
Retry two, retry three, and the third retry fails too.

00:04:37.12 --> 00:04:40.53
The project is Blocked, the callout explains why,

00:04:40.53 --> 00:04:45.81
the header offers Retry stage and Cancel, and the toast offers the way back.

00:04:46.16 --> 00:04:49.51
The Runs tab lists all four attempts, each one failed.

00:04:49.86 --> 00:04:52.24
Now the Owner presses Retry stage.

00:04:52.24 --> 00:04:55.67
Idea runs again from its start, passes this time,

00:04:55.67 --> 00:04:58.97
and the direction carries on to the next stage.

00:05:00.12 --> 00:05:01.65
Journey nine is complete.

00:05:01.65 --> 00:05:05.21
A failed run is retried three times, after thirty seconds,

00:05:05.21 --> 00:05:10.18
two minutes and ten minutes, and each retry is a new run that waits for its time.

00:05:10.18 --> 00:05:14.60
When the retries run out, the stage is Failed and the direction Blocked,

00:05:14.60 --> 00:05:19.14
with the reason and the last log lines, while other projects keep running.

00:05:19.14 --> 00:05:21.90
And Retry stage runs it again from the start.

00:05:21.90 --> 00:05:24.91
Next comes journey ten: queue further directions.
